 "literal": "What was undertaken without deviating those who die (acc.) who indeed failure those who can punish",
 "translation": "Who can find fault with and punish those who die without deviating from their vow?",
 "modern": "தாம் ஏற்றுக்கொண்ட சபதத்திலிருந்து விலகாமல் போர்க்களத்தில் உயிர்விடுபவர்களை, அவர்கள் தோற்றுவிட்டார்கள் என்று குற்றம் சாட்டித் தண்டிக்கக் கூடியவர் எவரும் இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "This couplet honors the ultimate commitment of warriors on the battlefield. The word 'iḻaittatu' refers to the solemn vow a soldier makes before battle, while 'piḻaittatu' refers to a failure or fault. If they fight to the death without retreating, they have fulfilled their vow perfectly. Therefore, even if the battle is lost, no one can punish or reproach them for failure, because their honorable death is itself a victory of their spirit.",
